ated crypto?\") but does the answer change which epics exist or what they do? The fix work is the same regardless — replace non-validated crypto in whatever images have it. That's more of an implementation detail / first phase of the implementation. The strategy states it as known fact (\"several images link non-validated crypto\"), not an open question. I'll fold the audit into the implementation epic scope (or as a first deliverable). Hmm, but one could argue an Investigation \"produce module inventory\" — actually the module inventory is part of the evidence package (HLR-2). I'll make the inventory a deliverable of the implementation/evidence work.\n\nEpic structure proposal:\n\nE001: KServe FIPS compliance implementation (component: KServe, team: RHOAI Serving, type: Implementation, P0) — audit KServe images for non-validated crypto, replace/patch with validated modules, verify correct operation on FIPS-enabled clusters. Covers HLR-1 (KServe portion).\n\nE002: Platform Operator FIPS compliance implementation (component: Platform Operator, team: ?, P0) — same for platform operator images. Covers HLR-1 (Platform Operator portion).\n\nWait — should KServe and Platform Operator work be one epic? Rule 1: different component → separate epics. Yes, separate.\n\nE003: CI FIPS regression enforcement (HLR-3, P1). Which component? The check gates \"any serving-stack build\" — spans both components. Per Step 2 component constraint: \"when one epic's work spans several strategy components, assign the single most-relevant canonical name and note the span\". Hmm. But also Rule 2's shared-surface enforcement deliverables rule: \"When a strategy mandates an enforcement deliverable over code surfaces — a lint rule, CI check, static-analysis check, or validation harness that guards a convention other epics of the same component implement — do NOT extract it into a standalone enforcement epic. Divide it per surface: each consuming epic's Scope carries the enforcement item for the surfaces it converts.\"\n\nHLR-3 is exactly that: a gating CI check that fails any serving-stack build reintroducing non-validated crypto. So the CI enforcement should NOT be a standalone epic — it divides per surface: the KServe epic owns the gating check for KServe builds, and the Platform Operator epic owns it for its builds. \n\nBut wait — the exception: \"This rule does NOT apply when the enforcement tool is the component's only implementation work (a strategy about building a linting/CI capability itself)\". Here the strategy is about FIPS compliance broadly, not about building a CI capability. So the enforcement divides per surface into E001 and E002. HLR-3 P1 gets bundled into the P0 epics — per Step 4, lower-priority HLR bundled: need to flag in HLR Traceability Matrix with \"(bundled)\" and rationale (shared surface, same call sites). HLR-3's check guards the same images the P0 work converts — same surface, so bundled. Good — this case is clearly testing exactly this rule (shared-surface enforcement deliverables). The validator \"check 6\" flags standalone enforcement epics.\n\nE003 (new numbering): Compliance evidence package + submission (HLR-2, P0). Component? It spans the serving stack. Assign most-relevant canonical: KServe (serving stack is KServe-centric) — note span. Team: RHOAI Serving. Type: Implementation. This assembles module inventory, build attestations, test results, and submits through the security review board to the certification lab. Depends on E001 and E002 (evidence of the compliant images). ACs must be on the deliverable: \"evidence package assembled and submitted\" — NOT \"certification obtained\" (externally controlled). Note the schedule risk (lab 6-9 months, review-board cadence) in Risks/Notes.\n\nShould the evidence package epic be separate from the implementation epics? Yes — different work-product type (compliance evidence assembly + submission process vs code changes), and it consumes outputs from both. Also the security revi