e Criteria\n\n1. A documented audit covers every KServe serving-stack image and identifies\n   all non-validated crypto library linkages (pass/fail: the inventory exists\n   and covers every image in scope).\n2. Every image identified by the audit is remediated so that no non-validated\n   crypto is used in the request path (pass/fail per image).\n3. The remediated images build successfully in the component's existing build\n   pipeline and produce build attestations.\n4. Verification results demonstrate that inference serving works correctly on\n   a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster with the remediated images.\n5. A gating CI check fails any KServe serving-stack build that reintroduces\n   non-validated crypto usage, and the check is wired into CI for those\n   builds (pass/fail: a deliberately non-compliant build fails the gate).\n6. The remediated images show no functional regression on non-FIPS clusters.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — KServe share: all KServe serving-stack images run correctly\n  on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules, with no\n  non-validated crypto in the request path.\n- **HLR-3 (P1, bundled)** — KServe share of the FIPS regression CI enforcement:\n  the gating check for KServe builds is carried in this epic because it guards\n  the same image builds this epic remediates (shared surface; not separately\n  schedulable from the remediation it guards).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Verification on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster requires access to such a\n  cluster; the acceptance criteria sit on the verification deliverables, and\n  cluster access is an environment prerequisite to be secured during\n  execution.\n- The exact set of affected images and libraries is unknown until the audit\n  runs; the remediation covers whatever the audit finds (fix-and-retry), which\n  is why no separate investigation epic is needed.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":484