{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b523d142ddc57631","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: E001/E002 remediation scope is unknown-bounded (the audit determines\n10\t    the work) and may exceed the ~2-week guideline, but Health Warnings record no\n11\t    size estimate or conditional re-split trigger; record the re-split trigger (e.g.,\n12\t    re-split by image group if the audit identifies more than N affected images) in\n13\t    Health Warnings\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n16\t  description: E003 AC5 closes with the lab acknowledging/triaging the submission,\n17\t    an outcome controlled by the external lab rather than the epic deliverable; end\n18\t    the AC at submission and move lab acknowledgement/triage to Risks/Notes\n19\terror: null\n20\t---\n21\t## Review Summary\n22\t\n23\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n24\tRecommendation: accept\n25\t\n26\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical\n27\tcomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type\n28\tparallelism, and shared-surface enforcement shape all pass). Triage is null (full\n29\tdecomposition), so the four-epic output is expected. No conditional branch files exist.\n30\t\n31\tThe decomposition is a clean translation of the strategy: two parallel per-component\n32\tremediation epics (HLR-1, with the HLR-3 CI enforcement folded per surface into each),\n33\ta P0 evidence-package epic (HLR-2), and a docs-authoring epic closing the DAG (HLR-4).\n34\tDAG diagram and frontmatter dependencies match exactly; the externally controlled\n35\tcertification process is correctly handled as risks rather than DAG edges or ACs. Two\n36\tminor issues are recorded; neither affects execution correctness.\n37\t\n38\t## Criterion Details\n39\t\n40\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n41\tAll four HLRs are mapped and the traceability matrix is present with no orphaned\n42\trequirements. HLR-1 is split by component boundary (E001/E002, both P0). HLR-2 maps to\n43\tE003 (P0). HLR-4 maps to E004, whose P0 priority correctly uses the docs-authoring\n44\texception (strategy priority Critical → P0), not the HLR's P2 label. HLR-3 (P1) is\n45\tbundled into E001/E002 — this is the rule-compliant shared-surface shape (the\n46\tenforcement deliverable divided per surface into the consuming epics), not a priority\n47\tcollapse: the CI check guards the very builds the P0 remediation converts and is not\n48\tseparately schedulable. The bundling decision is readable from the HLR-3 matrix row\n49\titself, satisfying the bundling-recording check. Priority inheritance holds: both root\n50\tepics are P0, and no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work.\n51\t\n52\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n53\tEvery diagram edge has a matching frontmatter dependency and vice versa\n54\t(E001→E003, E002→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004); arrows point from dependency\n55\tto dependent. No cycles, no invalid references, no missing data/artifact edges — E003's\n56\tconsumption of the audits, attestations, and verification results is edge-covered, and\n57\tthe build attestations consumed as content are correctly an acceptance criterion on\n58\tE003 rather than a DAG edge (the Rule 13 carve-out). E001 and E002 run parallel\n59\t(different components/repos, no shared artifacts). No konflux-onboarding,\n60\tlicense-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10/25 do not apply —\n61\tand kserve.md confirms KServe is already Konflux-onboarded, so no onboarding chain was\n62\towed. Critical path length 4 fits the M-strategy heuristic (3-4). Rule 11 docs-last\n63\tedges are present and correctly exempt from priority-inheritance flagging.\n64\t\n65\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n66\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: E001 (KServe) and E002 (Platform\n67\tOperator) are split along the component boundary; all components are canonical per\n68\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. E003 spans both strategy components in scope but is a\n69\tsingle logical deliverable (one evidence package, one submission per HLR-2) and carries\n70\ta component-mapping note — not a boundary violation. No scope duplication between\n71\tE001/E002 (each owns its own component's images and CI gate; no shared surface is\n72\tbuilt twice). No work-product type mixing: the docs deliverable is a separate\n73\tdocs-authoring epic. No standalone enforcement-tool epic exists — HLR-3 is correctly\n74\tdivided per surface into E001/E002, the shape the contract validator confirms. Team\n75\tassignments are inferred (RHOAI Serving for all four) but the inference is documented\n76\tin Health Warning 2 with a plan-time confirmation step. One minor: the remediation\n77\tepics have unknown-bounded scope (the audit determines the work) and may exceed the\n78\t~2-week guideline, but Health Warnings record no size estimate or conditional re-split\n79\ttrigger — record the trigger (e.g., re-split by image group if the audit identifies\n80\tmore than N affected images).\n81\t\n82\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n83\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces concrete artifacts (remediated\n84\timages + CI gates, evidence package, published documentation). Omitting Investigation\n85\tepics is justified and documented: the strategy declares no open questions on scope,\n86\tand the image/crypto audit resolves during implementation without changing which epics\n87\texist or what they do (fix-and-retry). No gated_by/gate_failure_impact fields are set,\n88\tand no epic lists an Investigation dependency, so the gating-metadata checks are\n89\tsatisfied vacuously.\n90\t\n91\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n92\tEach epic carries the type-appropriate `ai_signals` field (all are Implementation; no\n93\t`investigation_signals` misuse). Signal values were cross-checked against the\n94\tai-signals rationales and the architecture context: E001's `repo_access: 1` and\n95\t`architecture_claims: 1` are corroborated by\n96\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` (public midstream\n97\trepos, Stable/GA, Konflux-onboarded). E002's `repo_access: 0` and\n98\t`architecture_claims: 0` are correct — no context file covers Platform Operator\n99\t(verified: kserve.md is the only file in the tree), so the calibration rule does not\n100\tfire. E003's negative signals (`pattern_precedent: -1`, `existing_foundation: -1`,\n101\t`external_dependency: -1`, `human_process_gates: -1`) are each justified by the\n102\tstrategy content (no existing package, external lab, mandatory review-board\n103\tmilestones). E004's `architecture_claims: 1` correctly cites the serverless/raw\n104\tdeployment dependency facts in kserve.md. No calibration misses; computed\n105\tscores/thresholds were not re-derived per the review contract.\n106\t\n107\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n108\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are present:\n109\tE004 has the technical-review-against-implementation AC (Rule 24); the CI-gate ACs on\n110\tE001/E002 include a concrete failure test (a deliberately non-compliant build fails\n111\tthe gate); E003's package-content ACs gate the build attestations consumed as content.\n112\tThe externally controlled schedule constraints (lab turnaround, review-board cadence)\n113\tare correctly recorded in E003's Risks/Notes rather than as ACs, and FIPS-cluster\n114\taccess is handled the same way on E001/E002 — the ACs sit on verification deliverables\n115\t(\"results demonstrate correct operation\"), which is the valid shape. No epic body\n116\treferences sibling epics by draft ID (the E00X strings appear only in required\n117\tfrontmatter `dependencies` fields). One minor: E003 AC5's closing clause \"the\n118\tsubmission is acknowledged/triaged by the lab\" states an outcome controlled by the\n119\texternal lab rather than the epic's own deliverable — the AC should end at submission,\n120\twith lab acknowledgement/triage moved to Risks/Notes.\n121\t\n122\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n123\tThe combined epic set covers the full strategy scope: image remediation per component\n124\t(HLR-1), evidence package assembly and submission through the review board to the lab\n125\t(HLR-2), CI regression enforcement (HLR-3), and documentation of posture and\n126\tprerequisites (HLR-4). The problem statement's \"no compliance evidence package\" gap is\n127\taddressed by E003. Out-of-scope statements (workbenches, pipelines, training, Common\n128\tCriteria) are respected and mirrored in E004's limitations content. Cross-epic\n129\thandoffs are consistent: E001/E002's scope hands off module inventory, attestations,\n130\tand verification results, and E003's scope collects exactly those three. Strategy-level\n131\tcontext is carried forward, not dropped: the external schedule gates and FIPS-cluster\n132\taccess prerequisite appear as epic Risks/Notes, and the serving data plane is\n133\texplicitly addressed in both remediation epics' audit scope.\n134\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-922bf571b1b98224","agent_id":"a3a5f751399c1a3af","session_id":"f3804fb2-6446-43d6-8c27-86f0daf65430","uuid":"499a0083-4cb1-4b4f-88ac-ccd5c1cac08e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:16:35.845Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b523d142ddc57631","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
