{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-be6f0db7ac48d215","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n10\t\n11\tThe strategy is decomposed into four Implementation epics. Two parallel remediation\n12\tepics (one per affected component) make the serving-stack images FIPS-compliant and\n13\tcarry the per-surface CI regression gates; a compliance evidence epic assembles and\n14\tsubmits the certification package once the images are compliant; a documentation\n15\tepic closes the strategy. No Investigation epics are generated: the strategy\n16\texplicitly declares no open questions on scope, and the image/crypto audit does not\n17\tchange which epics exist or what they do (remediation covers whatever the audit\n18\tfinds — fix-and-retry, per the implementation-detail vs. genuine-unknown rule).\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 crypto module remediation for KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 crypto module remediation for Platform Operator images | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Document the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites for the model serving stack | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[\"E001 KServe image remediation (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 Evidence package + submission (P0)\"]\n34\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator image remediation (P0)\"] --> E003\n35\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 FIPS documentation (docs-authoring)\"]\n36\t    E002 --> E004\n37\t    E003 --> E004\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\tE001 and E002 are roots and run in parallel. The longest path\n41\t(E001 → E003 → E004, equivalently E002 → E003 → E004) contains 4 epics.\n42\t\n43\t## DAG Justification\n44\t\n45\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n46\t|------|------|-----------|\n47\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different components in different repos, no shared artifacts — the remediations are independent and must run in parallel. |\n48\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 | The evidence package is assembled from the remediation deliverables (module inventory, build attestations, FIPS verification test results) and must reflect the final compliant images, so it is serial after the KServe remediation. |\n49\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 | Same as above for the Platform Operator remediation — the package cannot truthfully describe a compliant serving stack until both components are remediated. |\n50\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; documentation describes what was built. |\n51\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n52\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n53\t\n54\tNotes on edges that deliberately do NOT exist:\n55\t\n56\t- No `konflux-onboarding` epic is generated, so Rules 7/8/10/25 do not apply.\n57\t  KServe is already Konflux-onboarded (per\n58\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`); the strategy\n59\t  does not ask to onboard any new repo or pipeline.\n60\t- Build attestations reach the evidence package as content via an acceptance\n61\t  criterion on E003 (\"the package includes the build attestations produced by the\n62\t  components' build pipelines\"), not via a DAG edge — the Rule 13\n63\t  configuration/content carve-out.\n64\t- The externally-controlled certification process (lab turnaround, review-board\n65\t  cadence) is recorded as risks on E003, never as DAG edges or acceptance\n66\t  criteria.\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n69\t\n70\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n71\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n72\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002 | Split by component boundary (Rule 1): KServe images vs. Platform Operator images. Both epics are P0. |\n73\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003 | Evidence package assembly and submission through the security review board to the external certification lab. |\n74\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001, E002 (bundled) | The gating CI check is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable: it guards the very image builds the P0 remediation converts. It is divided per surface — the KServe epic owns the check for KServe builds, the Platform Operator epic owns it for operator builds — because a standalone enforcement epic would be a work-type extraction and the check is not separately schedulable from the remediation it guards (same builds, same CI surfaces, splitting would force a second pass over each pipeline). |\n75\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | docs-authoring epic. HLR-4 is P2 in the strategy; the epic's P0 priority is derived from the strategy's overall priority (Critical → P0) via the docs-authoring priority exception, not from the HLR. |\n76\t\n77\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n78\t\n79\t## Health Warnings\n80\t\n81\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy provides no Affected Components\n82\t   detail table (components are a bare list, without per-component change or owner\n83\t   detail), no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then\n84\t   acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. The decomposition\n85\t   proceeded from the problem statement and HLRs; per-epic acceptance criteria\n86\t   were derived from HLR text. Human review should validate the derived ACs\n87\t   against the intended certification milestones.\n88\t2. **Team assignments are inferred.** The only documented owner in the provided\n89\t   context is RHOAI Serving for KServe\n90\t   (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`). All four\n91\t   epics are assigned to RHOAI Serving on the strength of the strategy's\n92\t   serving-stack scope; the Platform Operator and documentation ownership should\n93\t   be confirmed at plan time and the epics re-owned if different teams apply.\n94\t3. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** It is treated as an\n95\t   existing platform component (the strategy presumes its images exist and ship\n96\t   today — \"several images link non-validated crypto libraries\") rather than\n97\t   triggering a repo-onboarding chain. `architecture_claims` is scored 0 for that\n98\t   epic as no context file covers it. If Platform Operator is genuinely new to\n99\t   the platform inventory, re-decompose with the onboarding chain\n100\t   (repo-onboarding + license-validation → konflux-onboarding).\n101\t4. **External schedule gates are risks, not ACs.** Certification-lab turnaround\n102\t   (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are\n103\t   externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are\n104\t   recorded in E003's Risks/Notes; E003's acceptance criteria end at the\n105\t   engineering completion point the strategy controls (package assembled,\n106\t   milestone evidence delivered, package submitted and triaged). The\n107\t   certification grant itself is outside the strategy's engineering control.\n108\t5. **FIPS-enabled cluster access is an environment prerequisite.** Verification\n109\t   of remediated images on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster requires such a\n110\t   cluster; the ACs sit on the verification deliverables (results demonstrating\n111\t   correct operation), with cluster access noted as a risk on the remediation\n112\t   epics.\n113\t6. **Docs epic priority is not an inversion.** HLR-4 is P2 but E004 is P0 by the\n114\t   docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical`\n115\t   → P0). The docs epic does not push priority upstream (the implementation epics\n116\t   are already P0).\n117\t\n118\t## Tiered Delivery\n119\t\n120\tNot applicable as a DAG split. Tiered delivery applies when an external\n121\t*dependency* with uncertain timing gates downstream engineering work; here the\n122\texternal parties (certification lab, review board) gate the business outcome\n123\t(certification), not any engineering work item. Value nevertheless phases\n124\tnaturally: the FIPS-compliant images and CI regression enforcement (HLR-1, HLR-3)\n125\tdeliver user value independently of the certification timeline, and the\n126\tcertification (HLR-2) completes on the lab's externally controlled schedule. No\n127\tepic is blocked by an upstream external-dependency epic, so no Tier 1/Tier 2\n128\tsplit is warranted.\n129\t\n130\t## Component Mapping Notes\n131\t\n132\t- E003 (evidence package) spans both strategy components (KServe and Platform\n133\t  Operator); it is assigned the single most-relevant canonical component,\n134\t  **KServe**, because the evidence describes the model serving stack that KServe\n135\t  anchors.\n136\t- E004 is assigned the canonical component **Documentation** — the deliverable is\n137\t  user-facing documentation content.\n138\t- All component values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-922bf571b1b98224","agent_id":"a3a5f751399c1a3af","session_id":"f3804fb2-6446-43d6-8c27-86f0daf65430","uuid":"a29d2eb4-b43a-4d19-b73e-662902733cae","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:16:42.439Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be6f0db7ac48d215","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
